#!/bin/sh

# The commit gate.
#
# There is no build step in this repository on purpose — the folder is loaded into Chrome as
# it is — and no toolchain either: nothing here is installed, so this hook runs on a fresh
# clone with node and git and nothing else. That is deliberate. On a day with a clock running,
# a gate that needs `npm install` first is a gate that gets skipped.
#
# The hook is COMMITTED and `core.hooksPath` points at this directory (set by `npm install`,
# or by hand: `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`). A hook generated into a gitignored
# directory by an install is worse than none: a clone that was never installed runs no checks
# at all, silently, while looking exactly like a gated one.
#
# The file must stay mode 755. Executed directly, a 644 hook is skipped by git with only a
# hint that nobody reads.
#
# WHAT IT RUNS, and why it is one line rather than three.
#
# `npm run verify` is the same three checks this hook used to spell out itself — the manifest
# still describes a loadable extension (`scripts/check-manifest.mjs`), every script we wrote
# still parses (`scripts/check-syntax.mjs`), and the tests pass — and each of those files
# carries the reasoning for its own check at the top of it.
#
# It is spelled as the npm script rather than as the three commands because somebody who does
# not believe us runs `npm run verify` out of a clean clone, and a gate that is a SECOND
# spelling of that command is a gate that drifts away from it: the commit passes, the check the
# judges run does not, and nothing said so. One definition, in package.json, run from both
# ends. npm is not a dependency here — it arrives with node, which this hook already needs.

set -e

npm run verify
